Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Prologue", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.970916505744358*^9, 
  3.970916508558937*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"3b971b35-424e-4301-ab2d-\
7928dfbf81a4"],

Cell["\<\
A tangent vector to a curve is a vector that points in the instantaneous \
direction of motion along the curve . How you find it depends on how the \
curve is given . Below are the standard methods and quick recipes . \
Parametric curve r (t)
-Given : r (t) = (x (t), y (t), z (t)) for t in an interval . -Tangent vector \
: r\[Prime] (t) = (x\[Prime] (t), y\[Prime] (t), z\[Prime] (t)) . -Unit \
tangent : T (t) = r\[Prime] (t)/ || r\[Prime] (t) || provided r\[Prime] (t) \
!= 0.
-Example : r (t) = (t, t^2, ln t) \[DoubleRightArrow] tangent r\[Prime] (t) = \
(1, 2 t, 1/t) . Explicit planar curve y = f (x)
-Treat x as parameter : r (x) = (x, f (x)) . -Tangent vector : r\[Prime] (x) \
= (1, f\[Prime] (x)) . Any nonzero scalar multiple is also a tangent vector . \
-Unit tangent : T (x) = (1, f\[Prime] (x))/sqrt (1 + (f\[Prime] (x))^2) . \
-Slope form : derivative f\[Prime] (x) gives the slope of the tangent line . \
Implicit curve F (x, y) = 0 (planar)
-If \[PartialD]F/\[PartialD]y != 0, use implicit differentiation : dy/dx = \
-F_x/F_y . -Tangent vector : (1, dy/dx) or any scalar multiple (F_y, -F_x) \
(this is perpendicular to gradient \[Del]F = (F_x, F_y), so tangent) . \
-Example : circle x^2 + y^2 \[Minus] 1 = 0 \[DoubleRightArrow] \[Del]F = (2 \
x, 2 y), tangent vector = (2 y, \[Minus]2 x) \[Proportional] (y, \[Minus]x) . \
Level surface/surface intersection in R^3
-For surface S defined by G (x, y, z) = 0, tangent plane at p has normal \
\[Del]G (p) . -Tangent vectors are any vectors v with \[Del]G \
(p)\[CenterDot]v = 0.
-For a curve given as intersection of two surfaces G1 = 0 and G2 = 0, tangent \
direction is \[Del]G1*\[Del]G2 (cross product) evaluated at the point (if \
nonzero) . Parametrized implicitly in higher dimensions or by constraint
-For curve \[Gamma] (t) implicitly constrained by constraints, differentiate \
constraints and solve linear system for \[Gamma]\[Prime] (t) . -For example \
mechanical linkages : differentiate constraint equations to obtain velocity \
(tangent) vectors . Practical notes and pitfalls
-If r\[Prime] (t) = 0 at a point, first nonzero higher derivative may \
indicate direction (use Taylor expansion or reparametrization)\[LongDash]the \
point can be a cusp or stationary point . -Tangent lines use point p = r (t0) \
and direction r\[Prime] (t0) : line L (s) = p + s r\[Prime] (t0) . -Unit \
tangent and curvature : curvature uses T\[Prime] (t) normalized; if only \
direction needed, any nonzero scalar multiple of r\[Prime] is sufficient . \
Summary formulas

Parametric : tangent = r\[Prime] (t) . Explicit y = f (x) : tangent = (1, f\
\[Prime] (x)). Implicit F (x, y) = 0 : tangent = (F_y, \[Minus]F_x) or (1, \
\[Minus]F_x/F_y). 
(* https://www.quora.com/How-do-you-find-a-tangent-vector-to-a-curve *)\
\>", "Input",
